# Findings gitleaks reports in git history that are not secrets.
#
# One entry per finding, pinned to commit:path:rule:line -- never a rule,
# a path or a directory. A broad entry silences future real findings in
# the same place, which is how a scanner quietly stops scanning (#758).
#
# Adding to this file is not a way past a red build. A finding is
# investigated first, and an entry is written only once it is established
# that nothing needs rotating.

# Two recovery-key-shaped constants in the terminal-echo test, added in
# 6a358d7 on 2026-08-07 ("Keep recovery keys off every stream that
# persists them"). Established synthetic on 2026-09-03, per #758:
#
#   - They live only in test files, as `const key` here and as `testKeys`
#     in recovery_output_test.go, and appear nowhere else in the tree or
#     its history.
#   - Neither is ever verified against a recovery store. This test types
#     one into a pty and asserts the terminal did not echo it; the other
#     file formats three into expected CLI output. Nothing authenticates.
#   - A recovery key is only usable against a deployment that holds its
#     HMAC digest *and* that host's own pepper, which OpenRecovery
#     generates per host and deliberately keeps out of the digest store
#     (internal/auth/recovery.go). No digest and no pepper exists in this
#     repository, so these strings authenticate against nothing here.
#
# The working tree no longer carries them: both were replaced with
# obviously-fake placeholders in the same change, per AGENTS.md's rule
# that tracked examples never resemble working credentials. These two
# entries exist because history cannot be rewritten to remove what was
# already published, and the scan is deliberately over full history.
